Questions should not consist entirely of direct calculations.

Lesson 5 questions should measure four increasingly complex forms of understanding.

---

# 63. Level 1 — Recognize

Can the student recognize the concept?

Examples:

- identify a ratio, rate, or proportion from a description;
- identify whether a ratio is part-to-part or part-to-whole;
- identify which quantity a unit rate describes.

---

# 64. Level 2 — Execute

Can the student perform the skill?

Examples:

- simplify a ratio;
- calculate a unit rate;
- solve a proportion;
- convert a unit.

---

# 65. Level 3 — Apply

Can the student recognize and use the skill in context?

Examples:

- compare two grocery offers;
- scale a recipe;
- calculate wages from an hourly rate;
- find an actual distance from a map scale.

---

# 66. Level 4 — Reason

Can the student evaluate or explain a result?

Examples:

- determine whether a unit rate is reasonable;
- identify why a proportion setup was incorrect;
- explain which of two rates is the better value and why;
- determine whether a conversion factor was applied correctly.

