# 89. Real-Life Application Contexts

Prioritize contexts students can plausibly encounter.

Use:

- hourly wages;
- price per item or per ounce;
- grocery and retail comparisons;
- recipes and scaling;
- fuel efficiency;
- speed and travel time;
- map and blueprint scale;
- mixing ratios (paint, cleaning solutions, concessions);
- sports statistics;
- class or school ratios (students to teachers, wins to losses).

Avoid forcing artificial "real-world" contexts onto questions where a direct mathematical problem would be clearer.

